#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3de291a-f62d-568d-8c2e-8c6843e97fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275879} {:answer/id #uuid "44474fa0-b300-5a1d-bc71-c31fcab6ecca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275880} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e27484-80c6-525f-a22c-6f2a76cf2ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275881} {:answer/id #uuid "89f42cc9-9abe-5d2b-9453-325a37a2c5fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275882} {:answer/id #uuid "e54861c8-af68-5715-9891-48862e43054f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89f42cc9-9abe-5d2b-9453-325a37a2c5fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275882} :answer-field/id #uuid "7775113f-aabf-5a5f-90c4-2dd40846de46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae3f1372-2de8-5192-9ccc-ef2fc1ff02d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-11790" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(6,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(6,4),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(4) \\\\ 36 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05363ca3-93b9-55bc-8ab7-dda9c11c0fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=2(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275886} {:answer/id #uuid "21f91c6b-2db5-572d-afb6-f86984d26d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=4(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275887} {:answer/id #uuid "fcc946d8-6a13-5497-a02f-189e9847dad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=2(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275888} {:answer/id #uuid "a12332dd-a5b4-5fd8-8bf5-63eeb5fc083d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=-4(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275889} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3809ff-f2b5-545e-9414-4dee94505a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=-2(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcc946d8-6a13-5497-a02f-189e9847dad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=2(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275888} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc03ab09-fe27-590c-bd85-b5f8d0992319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab5a1b70-a3f1-5738-b055-95cc8bfcec5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-11822" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,-4)$ and which passes through the point $(1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-1,-4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-1))^{2} & =4p(y-(-4)) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (1+1)^{2} & =4p(-2+4) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y+4) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =2(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41067cb0-fbed-56c3-8885-c0fccaf92259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275893} {:answer/id #uuid "259888fb-579b-5072-8648-5b6b3798e8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275894} {:answer/id #uuid "24f88ff7-2937-5f35-86c6-7eb2bf033680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275895} {:answer/id #uuid "aee759b6-b6d2-5dd6-8b07-1eeb9d8cea24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275896} {:answer/id #uuid "1885a63b-a8ef-5e75-9b02-aea3a8bbf160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aee759b6-b6d2-5dd6-8b07-1eeb9d8cea24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275896} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f41cdd0-f785-5276-a2dd-e104ff774145" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc87e31d-742b-5368-879d-59099aad814b" :question/math-academy-id "q-11825" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,4)$ and which passes through the point $(4,-8).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(0,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,-8),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-8-4) \\\\ 16 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y-4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ebfafec-fe91-59c8-9712-a27db206390a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275900} {:answer/id #uuid "0b20a6ad-59e3-5654-afe8-d0b0c8adf5e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275901} {:answer/id #uuid "793c30b3-5402-591f-8570-ec252642ae9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275902} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ec1a6c-c904-575d-a89c-8d3adb38cefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275903} {:answer/id #uuid "374d3009-80c0-5e95-863c-a90e4e06cc50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b20a6ad-59e3-5654-afe8-d0b0c8adf5e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275901} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f9866c1-7043-5e63-8942-adf48f17c18b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9d40cb8-4907-57fa-84f1-cf5c810e6713" :question/math-academy-id "q-11835" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/assets/2819ceb4214f8df19ef349794c243771a1080bccf0bf2bbdbdbca9be67d4fbdb.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-4,6).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}=4p(y-6).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-6) \\\\ (4+4)^{2} & =4p(-6-6) \\\\ 64 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-6) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{4}{3})(y-6) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{3}(y-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b5c8fb-2950-5ba6-b916-fd24ad1c0a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6)^{2}=-9(y+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275907} {:answer/id #uuid "c05edaff-d14d-5f68-b54d-b88dd8a3873f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6)^{2}=3(y+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275908} {:answer/id #uuid "409d28b3-4e6b-5ff4-adb4-aa7f343ec8ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6)^{2}=9(y+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275909} {:answer/id #uuid "9d40308a-1963-50ad-9016-524971e93efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6)^{2}=-3(y+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275910} {:answer/id #uuid "038954da-ce71-5955-b007-6c26a619984e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}=9(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "409d28b3-4e6b-5ff4-adb4-aa7f343ec8ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6)^{2}=9(y+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275909} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d9411d9-ee67-5f36-ad76-207866aca62a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d43b4aa0-d2da-5bfe-b18c-4b320cf36d3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-11837" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13974277/assets/e72088788a2805da38d53f6c4d4080c6d91e10658d0d54c4b5953ce259e9637c.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-6,-6).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}=4p(y+6).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(0,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6)^{2} & =4p(y+6) \\\\ (0+6)^{2} & =4p(-2+6) \\\\ 36 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6)^{2} & =4p(y+6) \\\\ (x+6)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})(y+6) \\\\ (x+6)^{2} & =9(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
